description:
In ioctl_do_kdf() (src/cdev/crypto2dev_fd.c, lines 1725-1726):

    if (copy_from_user(&op, (void __user *)arg, sizeof(op)))
        return -EFAULT;   /* ← returns without clearing inbuf */

The caller writes IKM (input keying material — a secret) to the fd via write()
before calling DO_KDF.  This IKM is held in state->inbuf.

If copy_from_user() fails (invalid user pointer), the function returns -EFAULT
immediately.  state->inbuf is NOT zeroized and NOT freed.  The IKM remains in
kernel memory until the fd is closed.

FIPS 140-3 requirement (Critical Security Parameter zeroization): CSPs must be
zeroized on every exit path, including error paths.  A missed zeroization is a
FIPS compliance failure regardless of whether the memory is later freed.

Compare with ioctl_key_import() which was fixed this session (lines 1597-1603):
    if (copy_from_user(&op, (void __user *)arg, sizeof(op))) {
        mutex_lock(&state->lock);
        inbuf_clear(state);
        mutex_unlock(&state->lock);
        return -EFAULT;
    }

The same pattern must be applied to ioctl_do_kdf().

Note: the out_zero: label at line 1895 does call inbuf_clear, but it is NOT
reached from the copy_from_user early return at line 1726 — the 'return -EFAULT'
bypasses the goto chain entirely.

Fix: apply the same pattern as ioctl_key_import:
    if (copy_from_user(&op, (void __user *)arg, sizeof(op))) {
        mutex_lock(&state->lock);
        inbuf_clear(state);
        mutex_unlock(&state->lock);
        return -EFAULT;
    }

File: src/cdev/crypto2dev_fd.c, ioctl_do_kdf(), lines 1725-1726
